 "_readme": "This file is SOURCE, not generated output. It records a human decision made during tree merges: when two branch files independently invented the same technology under different ids, one id was kept as the survivor and the other was retired into it. The branch .json files still contain both spellings (deleting the losing side out of the branch file would throw away the branch author work of writing it), so treetool.py merge needs this mapping every run to know which of the two ids in front of it is the one to keep. Before this file existed the mapping lived only in tech_tree.json meta, which sim/treetool.py merge itself generates. That made the dedup record a generated-file-only fact: a rebuild of tech_tree.json from the branch sources alone, with tech_tree.json deleted, had no way to learn which ids were duplicates and would have resurrected every one of them as a second, separate node. Keeping the mapping here instead means the merge reads it as an input like any other branch file, and a full rebuild from data/branches/ reproduces the same tree. Format: merged_duplicate_ids maps a RETIRED id (the one dropped during merge, still present in branch source files) to its SURVIVOR id (the one that stays in tech_tree.json and that every reference to the retired id is rewritten to, including inside pre and req_any.options). Add an entry here, by hand, whenever branch review finds a new duplicate; never let treetool.py invent one - guessing which of two nodes with different ids describes the same technology is exactly the kind of silent decision the branch-merge authority rules exist to prevent.",
